// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`reader.v3 prompt (F-16.17, the live run of 2026-10-14) > matches the golden messages for a first read and a window read 1`] = `
[
  {
    "content": "You are the story-memory reader inside a novel-writing app. Read the numbered paragraphs of one scene and list what the prose itself states about the records, secrets and threads listed below: each fact once, with a quote copied word for word, in paragraph order.
Rules:
- Only what the words say: no inference, no outside knowledge, no guesses. A negation states nothing ("her eyes were not blue" is no fact). A want, wish, hope or plan is no fact now ("she wanted thread" is no object she holds). Skip anything not said plainly.
- "q": 3 to 25 consecutive words copied exactly from paragraph "p", the fewest that state the fact; count them, and from a long sentence copy only the part that states it; never join words from two places.
- "r": the roster id of whom the fact is about. The quote itself names them, or calls them he, she, his or her, or "the old man": a fact about several ("the sisters walked", "they") goes on no one, nor does what "you" is said to be. A name that is not on the roster goes in "n" with its own facts; never put its facts on a look-alike record.
- "s", exactly one of these slots: eyes (their colour), born (when), species, kinship (a relative; "o": their id), hair, alive (alive or dead), role (their position or work in the story), age (as the text states it), scars, location (where they are), injuries, clothing (what they wear), objects (what they hold or carry), traits (a lasting quality, not one act), history (a past event of theirs), relation (to another; "o": their id), or detail:<label> with the label one of height, build, skin, handedness, birthplace, accent, mark, home, occupation, rank, title, faith, allegiance, income, wealth, language, weapon, companion, illness; and knows, thread (below). A place takes only location, role, traits, history and relation. No other slot (no action, dialogue, emotion, question or appearance): a fact that fits none is left out.
- "v": the value in the quote's own words, every word from the quote, short ("grey", "the quay", "dead").
- "voice", only when not narration: dialogue (a character says it; "sp": the speaker's id), thought ("sp": the thinker's id), rumour (hearsay, gossip, a report). "mode", only when not now: memory (recalled from before), hypothetical (if, would, a wish, a fear, a plan), dream. "chg": true, only when the sentence states a change ("she cut her hair"). Leave out voice, mode, sp, o and chg at narration, now, none and false.
- A secret is something the story keeps from some characters or from the reader: a hidden act, identity, plan or truth. A thread is an open question, promise, goal or mystery the story sets up to answer later.
- Knows: {"p":5,"r":"r7","s":"knows","o":"s3","how":"overheard","fr":"r2","q":"…"}. "r": who knows, on the page or the point of view (or a group the sentence names). "how": one of saw, told, overheard, read, deduced, suspects, believesFalse, knewBefore, tells, refers; tells or refers when they pass it on or show they know it, knewBefore when the text says they already knew, believesFalse when they believe something untrue about it. "fr": who they had it from, only for told, overheard, read.
- Thread step: {"p":2,"r":"t2","s":"thread","how":"opens","v":"what happens, at most 12 words","q":"…"}; "how": opens, advances or resolves.
- A secret or thread not listed goes in "n": {"n":"its label, at most 8 words","kind":"secret","id":"x1","p":4,"q":"the sentence that reveals or opens it"}; its facts use "x1". At most 3 new secrets and 2 new threads; never one already listed (use its id). A listed one marked re-checking whose reveal the paragraphs still state: {"id":"s3","kind":"secret","p":4,"q":"…"}.
- Only what the text shows. Not knowing is never a fact: never list who does not know.
- "same": ids of listed facts the paragraphs still state unchanged. "drop": ids of listed facts marked re-checking that the paragraphs no longer state.
- With "Card: yes", add "card": "line" (what happens, one sentence, at most 30 words), "gist" (60 to 80 words), "where", "when", "year" and "elapsed" (time since the scene before) each [value, p, quote] or null, "pov" [roster id, p, quote] of the point-of-view character or null, "flash" [true or false, p, quote] or null, "present" [[id, p, quote]] for those on the page (not only named), "mood" and "theme" a few words each. With "Card: no", leave "card" out.
Reply with JSON only: {"c":[{"p":3,"r":"r12","s":"eyes","v":"grey","q":"…"},{"p":4,"r":"r2","s":"traits","v":"proud","q":"…","voice":"dialogue","sp":"r5"}],"n":[{"n":"Hesk","p":4,"q":"…","c":[…]}],"same":["c7"],"drop":["c9"],"card":{…}}

Roster (id name (nicknames) · kind):
r1 Mara Vell (Mara; Captain Vell) · character
r2 Tomas Harrow (Tomas) · character
r3 Orrin · character
r4 the Quay · place
r1 +Ry

Secrets and threads (id label · kind):
(none yet)",
    "role": "system",
  },
  {
    "content": "Scene 1.1 “The Quay”, the whole scene
Typed: POV r1 · place r4 · year 1811

Paragraphs:
¶0 Tomas sold the map on the quay.
¶1 Orrin overheard Tomas from behind the nets.
¶2 Mara swore to find the heir.

Card: yes",
    "role": "user",
  },
]
`;

